WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:19.769
My name is Hussein Fancy.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:21.146
My name is Allison Didychuk.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:22.564
My name is Binyam Mogessie.

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:23.606
I'm Tesca Fitzgerald.

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:24.941
I'm Nurfadzilah Yahaya.

00:00:24.941 --> 00:00:26.026
I am C.J. Argue.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:26.985
I am Mengxia Liu.

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:28.069
My name is Jane Lynch.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:34.451
I am a scholar of medieval Europe

00:00:34.451 --> 00:00:35.368
and the Islamic world.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.454
I work on herpes viruses, which are

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:39.414
incredibly common human pathogens.

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.916
I'm a historian of Southeast Asia.

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:43.209
My lab studies

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.336
fundamental mechanisms of human reproduction.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.380
My research is in robotics,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.757
interactive robot learning.

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:52.010
Ultimately, we want to make electronic devices

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.388
that can be used for energy applications in the future.

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.142
I specialize in Islamic law and colonialism

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.728
and I work with at least five languages

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:05.315
Arabic, French, Malay, English, and Dutch.

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:08.193
I'm a sociocultural anthropologist

00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:10.737
and what I'm particularly interested in is how

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.322
ethical and moral questions

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:14.866
emerge in the context of economic life.

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:16.701
I take people to the Middle Ages

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.369
because it is a foreign country for them.

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:20.538
It is a place that they will be detached

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:22.248
from everything they understand of the world

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.376
and return them to the present as changed people.

00:01:25.376 --> 00:01:28.713
We hope to make fundamental discoveries concerning the basis

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:30.507
of human infertility.

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:31.633
The virus that I work on

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.719
is called Kaposi's Sarcoma Associated Herpes Virus, 

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:36.763
which is interesting because it can cause cancer

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.723
in immune compromised individuals.

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:40.892
As a historian of Southeast Asia

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.852
and as someone who is actually from there,

00:01:42.852 --> 00:01:46.981
I think that I offer a unique perspective on the region.

00:01:47.732 --> 00:01:50.151
I'm in the Energy Science Institute

00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:53.196
and the idea of having that institute

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:56.324
is to gather faculty from different departments

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.494
who are working on energy applications

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:02.580
so people can work together and collaborate with each other.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.375
Do corporations have a social responsibility?

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:06.793
Should we try to create

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:10.255
social change through the things that we buy at stores?

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:13.842
These are the kinds of questions that I center my courses around

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:16.594
but also that emerge within the context of the research that I do.

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:19.639
An experience of math as a social activity,

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.308
appreciating that it's something that is best done

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:25.478
through collaboration and experimentation,

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.564
working with other people

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:29.023
and being willing to mess up together

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.192
and then get on the right track together will ultimately lead

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:33.862
to a much deeper appreciation for the material.

00:02:34.904 --> 00:02:37.740
What I hope to achieve from here is things that would

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.576
not have been possible in other places.

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:41.077
That is, scholarship

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:44.247
interactions and collaborations across different disciplines.

00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:48.376
We are working on a very interdisciplinary research

00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:49.878
that will combine

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:53.798
material science, chemistry and electrical engineering.

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:58.970
I hope to build a strong, happy lab full of scientists

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.305
from all walks of life.

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:02.307
I'm excited to teach.

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:05.602
I'm excited to expose some of the best students in the country

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:07.729
to the material that I find fascinating and interesting.

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:10.899
There's still a lot of unsolved problems out there.

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:12.192
And so what I really hope to do

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:14.777
is to take these natural curiosities and interests

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:16.988
that students have about A.I. and robotics

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:19.991
and turn that into questions that they want to solve.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.869
My hope here is not only to perform

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:24.495
cutting edge research,

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:27.248
but also to teach students from diverse backgrounds,

00:03:27.248 --> 00:03:28.166
to be a role model,

00:03:28.166 --> 00:03:29.250
to send a message

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.462
that this is really achievable for everyone.

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:33.630
I want them to learn

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.798
the way to solve a problem

00:03:35.798 --> 00:03:37.717
and how they think is very important

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.637
beyond the knowledge in the textbook.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:43.348
To encourage them to be attentive

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:45.808
to very ordinary things, to slow down

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.353
and become observers of the world

00:03:48.353 --> 00:03:49.062
around them

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:50.563
and things that seem

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.106
mundane or irrelevant,

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:54.317
but that end up being the small details

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.361
that open up very large worlds.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.614
What we're doing in the hour and a half that we're together,

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:02.242
we're cultivating a sense of how to understand and question

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:04.160
the world in front of us, and also how to shed

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.247
an alarming sense of certainty that so many people have

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.539
that they know what the world is.

00:04:08.539 --> 00:04:09.874
I think that becomes part

00:04:09.874 --> 00:04:12.043
of a lifelong journey of self understanding.

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:15.588
Research problems are problems that by definition

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.715
have not been solved by anyone before.

00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:19.759
And so there's going to be a lot of obstacles.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.427
So the biggest question isn't necessarily

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:23.096
how much do you know about this topic,

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:25.014
but rather how long are you willing to stick with this

00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:28.768
and continue to learn even after you make mistakes

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.395
and even after an approach doesn't work

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:32.647
until you come across the right solution?

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:34.482
I think that kind of stubbornness is ultimately

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:36.609
what makes a researcher successful.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.612
Being at Yale offers us the scientific tools and facilities

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:43.324
to perform our research at the higherst level.

00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:45.743
I was an undergraduate at Yale many years ago,

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:48.663
so part of the appeal was to return to a place that I love.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.125
What attracted me to Yale was the Yale History Department,

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:55.795
which is a top history department internationally.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.298
What attracted me to Yale is just how much

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.758
computer science and engineering are growing here.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:03.261
Everybody is so invested in teaching

00:05:03.261 --> 00:05:06.889
as much as they are in their own scholarship and learning.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.600
I feel like there's this very palpable sense that

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.396
learning here is like a connection to a lot of history

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:15.648
that has happened on this campus.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.775
There's a sense that there is more at stake here

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:20.486
than just the immediate task at hand.

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.780
And I'm very excited to be a part of that

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.783
kind of a community and connect with a bigger sense

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:26.951
of what we're really doing here.

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:30.121
All the faculty members and the chair and the dean,

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:34.083
they are very helpful and supportive of new faculty members

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:36.586
and they really want the junior faculty member

00:05:36.586 --> 00:05:38.671
to be successful at Yale.

00:05:38.921 --> 00:05:41.174
Part of what I loved about being at Yale

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:41.716
and part of what drew me back here

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:44.302
was that I I was an English major as an undergraduate.

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:46.596
I never took a history class as an undergraduate

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:48.973
but I've returned as a history professor.

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:50.475
And to me that says a great deal

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:52.101
about the kind of education I got here,

00:05:52.101 --> 00:05:53.561
The liberal arts themselves at Yale.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:54.896
And I'm excited to be a part of

00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:56.230
a community where we teach people

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.774
how to read, to think

00:05:57.774 --> 00:05:59.317
and the ways in which it imbues their lives

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:00.735
in really surprising ways.

